   "source": [
    "# Quiz: Techniques of Machine Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "> **On Alps:** These are the questions of this lesson's pre-lecture and post-lecture quizzes, from the course's quiz app, with the answers the quiz app accepts."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Pre-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-1-4-pre-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>When building a model, you should:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>prepare your data, then train your model</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. You collect and prepare your data first. Training the model comes later in the process.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>choose a training method, then prepare your data</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. You prepare your data before you choose a training method.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>tune parameters, then train your model</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Parameter tuning comes after you train and evaluate the model, based on how it performs.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-1-4-pre-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Your data's ___ will impact the quality of your ML model</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>quantity</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Quantity matters, but so does your data's shape. Both affect how well you can answer your question.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>shape</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Shape matters, but so does the quantity of your data. Both affect how well you can answer your question.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>both of the above</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Both the quantity of your data and its shape affect how well you can answer your question.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-1-4-pre-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>A feature variable is:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a quality of your data</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The key word is 'measurable.' A feature is a measurable property, often a column like 'date' or 'size'.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a measurable property of your data</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A feature is a measurable property of your data, often shown as a column heading like 'date' or 'size'.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a row of your data</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A feature is usually a column, like 'date', 'size' or 'color', not a row.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Post-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-1-4-post-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>You should visualize your data because</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>you can discover outliers</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>you can discover potential cause for bias</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>both of these</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-1-4-post-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Split your data into:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>training and turing sets</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The parts are called training and testing sets, plus sometimes a validation set. There is no 'turing' set.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>training and test sets</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. The training set is used to train the model, and the test set confirms how well it performs.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>validation and evaluation sets</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The main split is into training and test sets. A validation set is an extra part you may not even need.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-1-4-post-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>A common command to start the training process in various ML libraries is:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>model.travel</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Training a model is called fitting it, so the common command is model.fit.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>model.train</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Training is the idea, but the common command is model.fit, which 'fits' your data to create a model.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>model.fit</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Many ML libraries use model.fit, where you send in your features (X) and your target (y).</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  }
